  "query_preview": "..The upper pattern early this afternoon is defined\nby a weak trough across the Desert Southwest with a trough axis\nextending southwest from south-central Utah-Las Vegas-San \nDiego. The latest GOES-18 Channel 10 (7.34 um) lower-level \nwater vapor imagery (sampling between 750-500 mb) indicated a \njet streak making its way NE into extreme NW Sonora Mexico. \nStronger winds associated with this feature will make their way\ninto south-central Arizona this afternoon and then swing east \nthrough the evening. Given the strong mixing due to the ample \nsurface heating, expect breezy to locally windy southwest winds \n15 to 25 mph with gusts to 35 mph initially across central/eastern\nPima and Santa Cruz counties early this afternoon, spreading \neast into Cochise county later this afternoon. The combination\nof these winds and the low minimum RHs (below 15 percent), \nexpect near critical fire weather conditions this afternoon \nacross the southern portions of fire weather zones 151 and 152, \nespecially as the winds expand from Santa Cruz county into \nCochise county this afternoon. This scenario matches up well \nwith the elevated area defined on the Day-1 SPC Fire Weather \nOutlook for today. \n\nOtherwise, this trough exits the region Friday with another \nmore established trough making its way through the Great Basin \nthis weekend. Southeast Arizona will remain dry on the southern \nperiphery of this feature as it swings through Sunday and \nMonday...but we will see strong and gusty southwest winds across \nour neck of the woods. At this time, it looks like we will see \nSWLY winds 20-30 mph with gusts to 35-45 mph. The latest NBM 5.0\nprobability for near critical fire weather conditions (RHs<=15 \npct and 10-m winds 20+ mph) between 30-50 percent Sunday across\nthe majority of Southeast Arizona, with portions of southern \nCochise between 50-80 percent. If these probabilities persist \ninto tomorrow, we will likely pull the trigger on a Red Flag \nWarning for Sunday.",
